# GridSignals in-container scheduler (R3.1). The Dockerfile installs this file to
# /etc/cron.d/gridsignals; deploy/entrypoint.sh starts the cron daemon before it
# execs uvicorn. Debian /etc/cron.d format: m h dom mon dow USER command.
# Times are UTC (the image sets no timezone), matching R10.2.
#
# Four rules this file must keep, each pinned in tests/test_packaging.py because
# each one fails SILENTLY:
#
# 1. It drives deploy/ingest_pipeline.sh — the ONE canonical pipeline, shared
#    with the first-load entrypoint. A second hardcoded step list here would be
#    exactly the drift those tests exist to prevent.
# 2. Every job sources /etc/gridsignals.env first, and treats a failure to do so
#    as fatal. cron strips the environment, so GRIDSIGNALS_DB (a Dockerfile ENV)
#    and any optional API key are invisible to a scheduled run otherwise — which
#    would turn a deliberate skip-on-missing-key into a source that silently
#    never runs while the ledger reads healthy. The snapshot is written at
#    startup, mode 600, outside the repo tree: NO secret is ever assigned in this
#    file (R10.8).
# 3. Every job is wrapped in deploy/scheduled_run.sh, which serializes scheduled
#    ticks against each other and records a dated skip (exit 0) when a manual or
#    first-load ingest already holds the per-step ingestion lock (R3.2) — that
#    lock raises, it does not skip. Read the guard's header for what this does
#    NOT cover: the per-step lock is not held for the tick, so a manual run
#    started mid-tick can still interleave.
# 4. Each job is a brace group with the redirect OUTSIDE it, so a failure of the
#    env source or the `cd` — the two likeliest tick-killers — reaches the log
#    instead of cron's mail channel, which has no MTA in this image. Same reason
#    for MAILTO="".
#
# NOT scheduled here: the annual entity-identifier refresh (R4.2).
# app/enrich_entities.py writes reviewable seed CSVs to disk and never writes the
# store — generated fills are a reviewed-in-PR artifact by design. In a container
# those CSVs land on ephemeral storage and are never loaded, so a cron line here
# would only pretend R4.2 was automated. It stays an operator-run refresh
# (generate -> review -> python -m app.db.load_seeds).
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# Daily full refresh: licensing -> ingest -> classify -> score -> plays -> digest.
# This is what closes "a container with a durable volume never re-ingests": the
# first-load gate deliberately skips ingest when the volume already holds
# signals, so without a schedule the dataset is frozen at first boot.
17 3 * * * root { . /etc/gridsignals.env && cd /app && sh deploy/scheduled_run.sh sh deploy/ingest_pipeline.sh ; } >> /var/log/gridsignals-cron.log 2>&1
